    <h2 id="etsy-requirement">Does Etsy Require Sellers to Have a Privacy Policy</h2>

    <p>It depends on where you and your buyers are. Etsy's help article on shop policies says that if you are a seller based in the EU, or you offer items to buyers in the EU, you are required to create and comply with a privacy policy that meets the GDPR. For everyone else, Etsy recommends that all sellers create one, because many countries have similar laws.</p>

    <p>Etsy's Seller Policy (the version in effect since July 9, 2026) frames it the same way. Sellers must protect other members' personal information and comply with applicable data protection law, and that law may require you to post your own privacy policy where the people you deal with can read it. If you do post one, it has to be compatible with Etsy's Terms of Use, Seller Policy and Privacy Policy, so it cannot promise buyers something Etsy's rules forbid.</p>

    <p>The reason is that Etsy's own privacy policy only covers what Etsy does with data. Under EU data protection law, the Seller Policy treats you as an <strong>independent controller</strong> of the buyer information you obtain through Etsy. Once a buyer's name and address reach you for fulfillment, what you do with them (printing labels, keeping order records, replying to messages, passing details to a print partner) is your processing, and your policy is what explains it.</p>

    <p>In practice, even a US seller with no EU sales is in a better position with a policy than without one. State privacy laws such as the CCPA only reach businesses above certain thresholds, but buyers in the EU and UK can find and order from almost any shop that ships internationally, and a clear policy is the cheapest way to be ready when they do. If you are unsure which laws your buyer base triggers, <a href="/tools/privacy-law-checker/">a short checker that matches your audience against privacy regulations</a> settles it in a few clicks.</p>

    <h2 id="buyer-data">What Buyer Data Etsy Shares With You</h2>

    <p>Before you write anything, list what actually reaches you. For a typical shop it is this:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Order details.</strong> The buyer's name, shipping address, the items ordered, quantities, prices, and anything they typed into a personalization field or gift message.</li>
      <li><strong>Buyer email address.</strong> Depending on the order, Etsy may show you the buyer's email address, but since 2024 it has limited the buyer email addresses it passes to third-party apps that do not need them to fulfill an order. Etsy's own guidance is that you should never copy email addresses from Etsy orders into a mailing list.</li>
      <li><strong>Messages.</strong> When buyers write to you through Etsy Messages, you receive the message text, their Etsy username, and any photos or files they attach, which for custom orders can include sizes, names or other personal details.</li>
      <li><strong>Payment information: none.</strong> Etsy Payments processes cards, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay and Etsy gift cards on Etsy's side. You never see card numbers or bank details; you receive the order and a payout.</li>
      <li><strong>Shop statistics.</strong> Etsy Stats shows aggregated visits, traffic sources and conversion, not data about identifiable visitors. Etsy's old Google Analytics setting worked only with Universal Analytics, which Google retired in 2023, and Etsy has no native GA4 connection, so most shops no longer run their own analytics on Etsy.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Everything beyond that list is data you choose to collect: newsletter signups on your own website, Instagram DMs, order forms in Google Forms, or a print-on-demand app that receives order addresses. Each of those is a separate data flow your policy has to cover.</p>

    <h2 id="etsy-data-rules">Etsy's Rules on Using Buyer Data</h2>

    <p>Etsy's Seller Policy sets limits that are stricter than many sellers assume, and your privacy policy should not contradict them:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Use buyer data only for Etsy transactions.</strong> Email addresses, mailing addresses and similar information may be used only for Etsy-related communications or Etsy-facilitated transactions.</li>
      <li><strong>No mailing lists without consent.</strong> You may not add any Etsy member to an email or physical mailing list, or use their identity for marketing, without their consent. A purchase is not consent.</li>
      <li><strong>No promotions in Messages.</strong> Etsy Messages cannot be used for unsolicited advertising or promotions, or to contact someone who has asked you to stop.</li>
      <li><strong>You answer for your own disclosures.</strong> If you share buyer data without proper consent, including by accident, the responsibility is yours, not Etsy's.</li>
      <li><strong>Honor rights requests for data you hold.</strong> Outside Etsy's services you may have to respond to requests to access, correct, delete or object to the use of personal data you control.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>So if you want a mailing list, build it with an explicit opt-in (a signup form on your site or a checkbox the buyer ticks) and say in your policy which tool stores the list and how to unsubscribe.</p>

    <h2 id="what-to-include">What to Include in an Etsy Shop Privacy Policy</h2>

    <p>A good Etsy shop policy describes your data flows, not Etsy's. For a broader walkthrough of structuring each clause, see our <a href="/resources/how-to-write-privacy-policy-for-app/">guide to writing a privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Who you are.</strong> Your shop name, your name or business name, and a contact email. EU and UK law expects you to identify yourself as the controller.</li>
      <li><strong>What you collect and where it comes from.</strong> Order details from Etsy, message content, and any data you collect yourself (newsletter signups, custom order forms, social media).</li>
      <li><strong>Why, and on what legal basis.</strong> Fulfilling orders and answering messages is necessary to perform the contract. Keeping order records is a legal obligation (tax and accounting). Marketing emails rely on consent. Fraud prevention or improving your products may rely on legitimate interests. Vague phrases such as "to improve your experience" do not meet the GDPR's transparency standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Etsy Payments.</strong> State that Etsy processes payments and that you do not receive or store payment card or bank details.</li>
      <li><strong>Who else receives data.</strong> Name the services: shipping carriers and label tools (Etsy shipping labels, Pirate Ship, ShipStation, Shippo), print-on-demand or fulfillment partners (Printful, Printify, Gelato), email platforms (Mailchimp, Klaviyo, Flodesk), and bookkeeping software (QuickBooks, Wave, Craftybase). If a supplier makes or ships items for you, it receives the buyer's name and address and must be listed.</li>
      <li><strong>Etsy Ads and Offsite Ads.</strong> Etsy Ads promote your listings inside Etsy. Offsite Ads show them on Google, Facebook, Instagram, Pinterest, Bing, the Google Display Network and Etsy publishing partners. Etsy runs the ads and the click tracking under its own privacy policy; you receive no data about who saw or clicked an ad. A short note pointing buyers to Etsy's privacy policy for ad tracking keeps your policy accurate. For reference, Etsy charges a 15% Offsite Ads fee on attributed orders (capped at $100 per order) if your shop has sold under $10,000 in every 365-day period, and you may opt out under Shop Manager &gt; Settings &gt; Offsite Ads. Once a shop reaches $10,000 in any 365-day period, the fee drops to 12% and participation becomes mandatory for the life of the shop. Orders are attributed when a buyer clicks an ad and buys from your shop within 30 days.</li>
      <li><strong>Cookies and analytics.</strong> Etsy sets the cookies on its own pages, and its cookie banner manages consent. You only need your own cookie disclosure if you run analytics or ad pixels on a separate website or link-in-bio page.</li>
      <li><strong>International transfers.</strong> If you are in the EU or UK and use US-based tools, say that data is transferred outside your region and under what safeguard (the EU-US Data Privacy Framework or standard contractual clauses). Avoid older templates that still cite the EU-US Privacy Shield; it was invalidated in 2020.</li>
      <li><strong>Retention.</strong> Give real periods: order records for the time your tax rules require (often five to seven years), messages for as long as the shop is active, newsletter data until the subscriber unsubscribes.</li>
      <li><strong>Buyer rights and how to use them.</strong> Access, correction, deletion, objection, restriction and portability under the GDPR and UK GDPR; the right to know and delete under US state laws where they apply. Explain that requests about data Etsy holds go to Etsy, and requests about data you hold go to you.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="eu-uk-sellers">Extra Obligations for EU and UK Sellers</h2>

    <p>EU and UK shops carry more than the privacy policy itself, and most of it overlaps with the policy's content.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>A policy is mandatory, not optional.</strong> Etsy requires a GDPR-compliant privacy policy from EU-based sellers and from anyone offering items to EU buyers. UK sellers are under the UK GDPR, which mirrors the EU rules.</li>
      <li><strong>Trader details.</strong> Etsy marks business sellers who ship to the EU or EEA as "traders" and shows buyers your trader status, business registration number and business address. You edit these under Seller details on your public shop page (Shop Manager &gt; Sales Channels &gt; your Etsy shop). Your privacy policy's contact section should match them.</li>
      <li><strong>Consent for marketing.</strong> EU and UK e-privacy rules require express consent before you send marketing emails, including to buyers whose email you received for an order. Etsy's legal team makes the same point in its Seller Handbook guidance on privacy policies.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-EU sellers with EU buyers.</strong> The GDPR can apply to a US, Canadian or Australian shop that offers goods to people in the EU. Such businesses may also need an EU representative, though there is an exemption for occasional, low-risk processing; our guide to <a href="/resources/gdpr-eu-representative/">when a non-EU business needs a GDPR representative</a> covers the test.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep consumer-law terms separate.</strong> The 14-day right of withdrawal and delivery rules come from EU consumer law, not the GDPR. They belong in your returns policy, not your privacy policy.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If you also sell from your own storefront alongside Etsy, that site needs its own policy covering checkout, cookies and payment processors directly; our walkthrough of <a href="/resources/privacy-policy-for-ecommerce-website/">what an online store's own privacy policy has to disclose</a> covers the extra clauses.</p>

    <h2 id="how-to-add">How to Add a Privacy Policy to Your Etsy Shop</h2>

    <p>Etsy keeps the privacy policy in its own tab of your shop policies. Shop policies cannot be edited in the Etsy Seller app, so use a desktop browser or Etsy's mobile website.</p>

    <ol class="steps-list">
      <li><strong>Open Shop Manager.</strong> Sign in on Etsy.com and open Shop Manager.</li>
      <li><strong>Go to Settings, then Policy settings.</strong> The policy page has tabs for Returns &amp; exchanges, Cancellations, Privacy, and Etsy's fixed policies.</li>
      <li><strong>Open the Privacy tab and select Create a policy.</strong> If you already have one, edit it instead.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose your text.</strong> Etsy offers its sample privacy policy to customize. To use your own, replace the sample with the policy you generated or adapted from the template below.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish.</strong> Select Publish. The policy appears in the Policies section of your shop page, where buyers can read it before ordering.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>It also helps to host the same policy at its own URL. Etsy's Privacy tab is only visible on Etsy, but you may need a link elsewhere: under a newsletter signup form, in an Instagram or TikTok bio shop, or in the footer of your own site. A hosted copy keeps one version in sync across all of them.</p>

